when I was growing up going to Grandma's  house was always a huge treat and that  we got to go rollerblading in the  basement make a mess and not clean it up  watch old musicals gramps taped on TV  eat freshly fried chips and of course  big bath crisp apple crisp is my  grandma's specialty and judging by how  much we loved it and how often been over  there pretty much just to get it she  acquired quite a few opportunities to  perfect it and I think September was  probably the best month to go visit  grandma cuz it often meant a trip to the  or through as part of the tradition now  apple picking with my family was one of  my first true connections with my food  and was really an adventure in itself I  remember staring up at the sky through  the branches at these arms the size of  my head all the biggest and brightest  apples were at the top where I couldn't  reach so we'd have to get hoisted into  the air and reach with our little arms  to grab them there were a lot of eating  breaks in the course of our day which  really stretched out the whole  experience it's nice you get a really  good wine and you do want to put it in  the bag in case god forbid Oh get lost  and hit up in someone else's lunch so  there's a lot of sampling eventually  with full bags and bellies we all pile  into the car and make a way to Grandma's  house and I remember the pride I felt  standing outside grandma's front door  with an overflowing bag of personally  selected gems I picked these apples for  and I knew the reward was going great  grandma used to let us get as involved  in the kitchen as possible I stand up on  this booster seat and she screw an apple  on to the old rotary peeler and I watch  as the peels would flow down like pretty  little red ribbons I was the only one  that actually liked eating the peels of  course so that was my little sous chef  treat when's peeled and sliced she loves  to toss him in as much cinnamon sugar as  we wanted it on with the crumbly topping  and after what seemed like a lifetime in  the oven it would emerge out golden and  toasty brown my grandma's no longer with  us but the memory of hand selecting  those ingredients and making and eating  that apple crisp with her remains with  me if I close my eyes I can still taste  all those ottimo flavors the apples were  tender and perfumed with the scent of  comfort coded so completely in  caramelized sugar that created this  chewy texture between my teeth the  topping was crumbly and crunchy and  supple and satisfyingly sweet  I used to try to mimic what grandma did  but have since given up the pursuit yes  I know grandma's recipe contain apples  and oats and cinnamon and sugar and  butter and I probably have a good idea  of the amounts but grandma's apple crisp  was also baked with an enormous amount  of a very specific love that I've never  been able to duplicate I might revisit  my mission one day when I have grandkids  in my own but until then I'm simply  happy to relish in my memory of grandma  and are irreplaceable connection through  food so thanks for joining me for  today's gastronomic adventure be sure to  check back regularly for upcoming  episodes of Abby's kitchen where nothing  says lovin like selling from the oven  you
 

